1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
And now to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:10,540
There's three ways for a Scoutmaster to get something done.

3
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:12,920
Naturally, they can do it themselves.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:14,340
That's the first way.

5
00:00:15,700 --> 00:00:19,760
Second, they can request that the troop committee take care of it.

6
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:26,400
And third, is to tell the Scouts that they're absolutely forbidden from doing it.

7
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:29,280
Yeah, that'll get it done, won't it?

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,040
Welcome back.

9
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:33,580
This is podcast number 14.

10
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:35,100
Hey!

11
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:35,580
Nice.

12
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:37,280
Hey!

13
00:00:37,280 --> 00:01:00,180
Hi, welcome back to the Scoutmasters podcast.

14
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:01,960
My name is Clark Green.

15
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:03,940
You know what I managed to do this week?

16
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,820
I managed to kill my Twitter account.

17
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:11,740
That is a sentence I never thought I would ever hear myself say.

18
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:13,780
I killed a Twitter account.

19
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:17,140
Yeah, I had a Twitter account at Scoutmasterblog.

20
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:22,780
And I was doing something, and somehow I smashed it.

21
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:25,860
And I'm not really quite sure what happened.

22
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:32,440
But now I have a Twitter account called ScoutmasterCG.

23
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:34,440
ScoutmasterCG.

24
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:39,620
So if you want to go back to following me on Twitter, you're going to have to have heard

25
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:41,759
this and then go back to ScoutmasterCG.

26
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:43,560
I'm really sorry.

27
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:45,520
I don't know how it happened.

28
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:47,039
Oh, well.

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,420
Hey, this week on the podcast, we're going to start off with Scoutmastership in seven minutes

30
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:53,820
or less.

31
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:57,880
And we're going to talk a little bit about training, trusting, and letting as far as it

32
00:01:57,880 --> 00:01:59,000
concerns our youth leaders.

33
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:05,980
Then I have an interview with author Richard Bennett, who has written a book called Everything

34
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:08,020
I Needed to Know I Learned in Boy Scouts.

35
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:12,400
And there'll be information on the blog as to where you can buy the book.

36
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:14,960
It's well worth it, in my humble opinion.

37
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:15,760
Well worth it.

38
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:20,680
Then we'll finish up with our third installment of Avoiding Eagle Scout drama.

39
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:24,040
So that's a pretty full slate.

40
00:02:24,740 --> 00:02:25,720
Let's get ourselves to it.

41
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:29,760
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

42
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:38,460
Good old Bill Hillcourt.

43
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:39,960
Gosh, what a guy.

44
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:46,060
He wrote the first patrol leader's manual and is famous for having said, train them,

45
00:02:46,180 --> 00:02:47,240
trust them, and let them lead.

46
00:02:47,320 --> 00:02:52,260
Now, there's a formula for a youth-led organization, a youth-led troop.

47
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:55,140
Train them, trust them, let them lead.

48
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:55,680
Training.

49
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:57,680
Yeah, they need to be trained.

50
00:02:57,820 --> 00:03:04,680
Now, one really strong training method is not necessarily to do like a two-week junior

51
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:07,220
leader training thing or a weekend or something like that.

52
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:13,120
It's called OJT, on-the-job training, as opposed to JLT, junior leader training.

53
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:22,600
Where there's a decent example of active and engaged youth leadership in a troop, what I find

54
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:28,840
to be particularly effective is to train your new leaders using the existing leaders, using

55
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:31,220
the experiences, and do it right on the job.

56
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:33,700
And that seems to work very, very well.

57
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:38,360
As a matter of fact, that's worked well in my troop for a number of years now.

58
00:03:39,060 --> 00:03:45,640
If you don't have that established, then it might be necessary to go to some kind of a

59
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:50,980
protracted youth leader training event and to give the guys a real good picture.

60
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:57,700
The other thing to do is to observe a troop with a well-developed youth leadership.

61
00:03:59,220 --> 00:04:03,399
But training and having that is the most important thing.

62
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:06,739
Training, to me, is coaching and mentoring.

63
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,760
It's a constant thing in the life of the troop.

64
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:14,299
It happens in that five minutes before the meeting and that five minutes after the meeting

65
00:04:14,299 --> 00:04:19,640
in a little huddle on a camping trip where you're going to mentor and suggest actions

66
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:21,000
and ask a lot of questions.

67
00:04:21,539 --> 00:04:21,899
Training.

68
00:04:22,219 --> 00:04:24,000
Training never stops.

69
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:25,280
Trusting.

70
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:28,680
Wow, it's hard to trust youth leaders to begin with.

71
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:29,880
It's very, very difficult.

72
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:31,400
But I'll tell you something.

73
00:04:31,719 --> 00:04:38,979
The more responsibility they have, the more latitude they are given, the better they perform.

74
00:04:38,979 --> 00:04:41,760
And I can't stress that enough.

75
00:04:42,180 --> 00:04:44,400
That is actually the way that it happens.

76
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:45,760
Give them responsibility.

77
00:04:46,539 --> 00:04:47,620
Give them a lot of latitude.

78
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:50,320
Give them decision-making power.

79
00:04:50,780 --> 00:04:53,420
Give them authority to do what they need to do.

80
00:04:53,800 --> 00:05:00,700
And they will absolutely shock you with their skill and the ability that they have to take

81
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:01,920
those things on and do them.

82
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:04,840
But trusting, it's not easy.

83
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:08,820
Once you've jumped off the cliff, you know, you better start to fly.

84
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:15,560
And that is actually the feeling that you get the first few times that you have to go through

85
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:21,659
trusting youth leaders to really take over and do the job of running their troop.

86
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:24,680
Training, trusting, and letting them lead.

87
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:26,840
Letting is a pretty important thing.

88
00:05:26,940 --> 00:05:29,060
It's pretty close to trusting.

89
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:34,320
But it's something that you have to renew every single time you function as a scout leader.

90
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:36,099
You have to let them lead.

91
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:37,400
You have to let them lead.

92
00:05:37,560 --> 00:05:38,719
You have to let them lead.

93
00:05:39,159 --> 00:05:40,680
You can't stand in their way.

94
00:05:40,820 --> 00:05:47,340
You can't constantly be, you know, coaching and mentoring and calling them apart and micromanaging

95
00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:50,840
in that way every single little thing they do.

96
00:05:51,020 --> 00:05:52,700
You have to let them go.

97
00:05:53,060 --> 00:05:53,719
Let them lead.

98
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:54,740
They'll make mistakes.

99
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:57,020
There'll be difficulties along the way.

100
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:58,060
But you know what?

101
00:05:58,180 --> 00:06:00,219
They're going to do just fine.

102
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:00,860
Train them.

103
00:06:01,039 --> 00:06:01,480
Trust them.

104
00:06:01,620 --> 00:06:02,159
Let them lead.

105
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:03,440
Bill Hillcourt wrote that.

106
00:06:03,540 --> 00:06:05,620
Oh, it's got to be 60 years ago now.

107
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:12,060
But it has proven itself over time in scout troops all over the place.

108
00:06:12,220 --> 00:06:14,960
And even with the one that you're going to meet with this week.

109
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:35,539
I'm talking to Richard Bennett from Irving, Texas.

110
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:41,960
And Richard has written a book called Everything I Needed to Know I Learned in Boy Scouts.

111
00:06:41,960 --> 00:06:46,060
And it's the story of his Boy Scout troop, Troop 826.

112
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:48,979
I have really gotten attached to this book.

113
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:53,859
And I'm very happy to be interviewing Richard from his home in Irving today.

114
00:06:53,919 --> 00:06:54,659
How are you doing, Richard?

115
00:06:54,900 --> 00:06:55,440
Doing fine.

116
00:06:55,539 --> 00:06:56,060
Thank you, Clark.

117
00:06:56,159 --> 00:06:57,780
Tell you what I really like about this book.

118
00:06:57,900 --> 00:06:58,859
It is honest.

119
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:06,359
The first thing I wanted to do was to tell the story about the troop and the scoutmaster, Mr. Street, Warren Street.

120
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:10,620
And then I wanted to talk about the boys that I was in the troop with.

121
00:07:10,740 --> 00:07:11,520
They were my friends.

122
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:17,180
And then beyond that, it was basically the story of the Boy Scouts itself.

123
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:22,240
And what a good program it was for us kids who were growing up.

124
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:24,680
So tell me a little bit about Irving about 40 years ago.

125
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:27,720
Well, Irving is a suburb of Dallas.

126
00:07:28,180 --> 00:07:30,180
And it used to be country.

127
00:07:31,240 --> 00:07:35,900
And we used to have fields out here and rolling plains.

128
00:07:36,340 --> 00:07:46,040
And in fact, Irving was right next to a ranch, the Carpenter Ranch, which is all now Las Colinas and Valley Ranch, where the cowboys have their training field.

129
00:07:46,340 --> 00:07:49,600
But when I was a kid, that was all farmland.

130
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:51,140
Here you are growing up in Irving.

131
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:55,080
And what got you interested in the scouts at the time?

132
00:07:55,800 --> 00:08:04,360
Basically, my big brother, my big brother David, when he went into something, I, of course, I wanted to do it also, you know, because I didn't want him to have all the fun.

133
00:08:04,760 --> 00:08:05,920
He got into Boy Scouts.

134
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:09,600
We were in Cub Scouts together, but then he got into Boy Scouts when he was 11.

135
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:17,260
And I had to wait for two years, you know, and listen to his stories, telling about campouts and the adventures they'd have.

136
00:08:17,260 --> 00:08:20,880
And I'd even go up to scout meetings at night.

137
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,099
I was at a local schoolhouse.

138
00:08:23,460 --> 00:08:28,260
And so I would go up there and watch the boys have fun through the windows.

139
00:08:28,640 --> 00:08:30,640
And I thought, man, this is what I want to do.

140
00:08:30,840 --> 00:08:32,900
So when I was 11, I joined.

141
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:37,659
And I wasn't the best scout, but I was okay.

142
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:38,539
I guess I was typical.

143
00:08:38,980 --> 00:08:40,880
That's one of the things I really like about this, Richard.

144
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:45,680
That's the voice of, you know, 90% of the scouts that go through this program.

145
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:51,540
Well, I just, I would just try to tell the truth as I saw it and as I remember it.

146
00:08:51,959 --> 00:08:54,579
And, you know, now I'm in my 50s.

147
00:08:55,500 --> 00:09:00,579
I look back on a lot of the bright spots in my life, and that was one of them.

148
00:09:00,880 --> 00:09:03,959
And I quit, you know, way too soon.

149
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:05,839
And now I'm going, what was I thinking?

150
00:09:07,300 --> 00:09:11,020
That was the most fun part of my teenage years, I think, you know.

151
00:09:11,699 --> 00:09:14,240
Tell me a little bit about Mr. Street, your scoutmaster.

152
00:09:14,240 --> 00:09:16,220
Mr. Street, Warren Street.

153
00:09:16,920 --> 00:09:20,680
Well, he's what you call a rugged individualist.

154
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:24,580
And World War II came along, and he joined the military.

155
00:09:24,940 --> 00:09:26,160
And he made a career out of it.

156
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:31,600
In his 50s, he was in better shape than we were when we were in our adolescent years.

157
00:09:31,940 --> 00:09:32,240
Yeah.

158
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:37,660
So, and he was, and he loved camping, you know, and so that was his thing.

159
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:40,140
He loved camping, and he liked having fun with the kids.

160
00:09:40,860 --> 00:09:42,400
And he looked out for us.

161
00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:45,160
He was a second dad to a lot of us.

162
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:49,000
I think that's why some of the kids latched on to him like they did, you know.

163
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:51,200
He was like a second dad.

164
00:09:51,340 --> 00:09:53,180
Me and my brother came from a pretty stable home.

165
00:09:53,180 --> 00:09:57,420
We really didn't have a real need for a second dad.

166
00:09:57,660 --> 00:09:59,500
But it was nice that he was there.

167
00:10:00,339 --> 00:10:06,260
Now, did some of that military experience that he had carry over into his job as a scoutmaster?

168
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:08,819
Well, you know, he didn't push it.

169
00:10:09,020 --> 00:10:13,359
But there was a kind of military bearing, you know.

170
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:17,260
But he never did really, he never bragged about his days in the military or anything.

171
00:10:17,459 --> 00:10:18,319
He never talked about it.

172
00:10:18,319 --> 00:10:18,479
Right.

173
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:19,840
Because we wouldn't have understood it.

174
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:21,960
Mr. Street had a good sense of humor.

175
00:10:22,540 --> 00:10:26,080
He wasn't what you'd call a jokester, but he was a prankster.

176
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:26,500
Uh-huh.

177
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:28,600
He would pull pranks on us every now and then.

178
00:10:28,980 --> 00:10:33,060
You know, and there was a story in the book about the radiation man.

179
00:10:33,220 --> 00:10:33,640
Oh, right.

180
00:10:33,700 --> 00:10:35,680
I was going to say radioactive man, but you're right.

181
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:36,640
Radiation man.

182
00:10:36,720 --> 00:10:36,960
Right.

183
00:10:37,420 --> 00:10:41,920
We kids, we'd get out of our tents at night and go, you know, try and break the rules.

184
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:46,260
So he would prowl the grounds at night in his radiation garb outfit.

185
00:10:46,260 --> 00:10:49,540
And this was after telling the story of radiation man by the campfire.

186
00:10:49,700 --> 00:10:50,800
I love that part of the book.

187
00:10:50,860 --> 00:10:51,640
I love that story.

188
00:10:51,819 --> 00:10:52,020
Oh, yeah.

189
00:10:52,480 --> 00:10:59,360
In 2006, a bunch of members of Troop 826 got back together for a reunion.

190
00:10:59,520 --> 00:10:59,939
That's right.

191
00:11:00,480 --> 00:11:02,640
We'd had a few birthday parties for Mr. Street.

192
00:11:02,819 --> 00:11:03,700
You know, he's still alive.

193
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:05,020
He's 87 now.

194
00:11:05,220 --> 00:11:05,560
Uh-huh.

195
00:11:05,560 --> 00:11:10,140
But when he started hitting the 80s, you know, we started saying, you know, let's get back together.

196
00:11:10,359 --> 00:11:15,000
And Mike Quine, an Eagle Scout, he put together a website of our troop.

197
00:11:15,239 --> 00:11:17,420
And we all gathered one time for his birthday.

198
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:23,020
And we had a birthday cake for him and surprised him and showed him the website.

199
00:11:23,260 --> 00:11:27,239
And then in 2006, we had a reunion campout.

200
00:11:27,660 --> 00:11:30,599
And we went on this campout, and Mr. Street did all the cooking for us.

201
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:34,240
Well, he already knew how you guys cooked, and he wasn't about to let you do that, right?

202
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:35,480
That's probably right.

203
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:36,200
You're probably right.

204
00:11:36,420 --> 00:11:41,740
I saw how the stories that were told there at the campout and people reminiscing about their days in scouting.

205
00:11:42,060 --> 00:11:43,600
And I thought, this means something.

206
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:46,020
It means a lot to these men, grown men in their 50s.

207
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:52,280
It's hard to know until you're a bit older what those experiences do to shape your life.

208
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:53,600
You don't really understand it.

209
00:11:53,880 --> 00:11:54,580
No, you don't.

210
00:11:54,940 --> 00:11:55,180
I didn't.

211
00:11:55,180 --> 00:11:57,380
Tell me a little bit about your first campout.

212
00:11:57,480 --> 00:11:58,640
That's always pretty memorable.

213
00:11:58,640 --> 00:12:01,540
My first campout was my summer camp.

214
00:12:01,699 --> 00:12:04,939
I went to Camp Wisdom when I was 11 years old in 1968.

215
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:09,840
And, of course, I was tagging along with my big brother, and I got in his way.

216
00:12:09,920 --> 00:12:10,340
No doubt.

217
00:12:11,620 --> 00:12:17,080
But I was there, and that was my first experience being around with the older guys.

218
00:12:17,699 --> 00:12:19,520
And that was my first time to be away from home.

219
00:12:19,939 --> 00:12:22,240
And so it was a little tough on me emotionally.

220
00:12:22,840 --> 00:12:25,520
That was like a milestone.

221
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:29,460
And the rest of the campouts were fairly easy compared to that.

222
00:12:29,780 --> 00:12:31,860
But at a summer camp, you don't have to cook for yourself.

223
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:34,199
Well, that's right.

224
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:37,340
So that takes one of the impediments away.

225
00:12:37,460 --> 00:12:38,220
Yeah, that's true.

226
00:12:38,340 --> 00:12:42,199
If you do it in mine, Richard, there's a piece about your first campout.

227
00:12:42,340 --> 00:12:43,300
Would you mind reading that?

228
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:44,300
First campout.

229
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:47,180
Things don't work in the country like they do in the city.

230
00:12:47,180 --> 00:12:55,160
There's no heating or air conditioning, no beds, no mother to cook, no father to tell us what to do next, no television, and no telephones.

231
00:12:55,620 --> 00:12:59,400
Most of us weren't used to taking orders from anyone outside the family or classroom.

232
00:13:00,199 --> 00:13:05,359
Here, we had to learn teamwork and cooperation, or else it wasn't going to be an easy time.

233
00:13:06,120 --> 00:13:08,199
Your first week at Camp Wisdom.

234
00:13:08,459 --> 00:13:08,719
Right.

235
00:13:08,780 --> 00:13:11,560
What responsibilities did you have, and how did you approach it?

236
00:13:11,560 --> 00:13:15,699
My responsibility was just lay low, stay out of trouble, and watch.

237
00:13:17,599 --> 00:13:19,599
Because I didn't know what was going on.

238
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:20,560
I had no clue.

239
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:26,439
But here I am around a guy who's just a year or two or three years older than I am, telling me what to do.

240
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:28,099
And I had a hard time with that.

241
00:13:28,439 --> 00:13:31,380
Were you the youngest guy in the bunch at the time?

242
00:13:31,400 --> 00:13:32,160
At the time, I was.

243
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:34,979
Oh, and you're somebody's little brother to boot.

244
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:35,260
Right.

245
00:13:36,099 --> 00:13:38,260
Oh, well, see, that's not easy for anybody.

246
00:13:38,380 --> 00:13:39,640
Oh, and I got picked on.

247
00:13:39,640 --> 00:13:42,100
And David, everybody liked David, my big brother.

248
00:13:42,300 --> 00:13:43,960
You know, he's the life of the party.

249
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:45,080
Right.

250
00:13:45,160 --> 00:13:48,980
If somebody is having a hard time with David, they're not going to take it out on David.

251
00:13:49,060 --> 00:13:49,880
They'll take it out on me.

252
00:13:51,960 --> 00:13:56,620
So, you know, I got to hear all the, you know, how I don't like your brother stories, stuff like that.

253
00:13:57,020 --> 00:14:03,240
The book is a lot of your own recollections, and then recollections from a lot of your fellow scouts.

254
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:03,740
That's right.

255
00:14:03,740 --> 00:14:12,360
The thing that I find kind of amazing is everybody remembers what it was like to be 11, 12, 13, 14 years old.

256
00:14:12,420 --> 00:14:13,800
They remember that pretty clearly.

257
00:14:14,340 --> 00:14:21,380
And they remember they can look back through the eyes of that boy and at the experiences and talk about it still.

258
00:14:21,380 --> 00:14:23,420
Well, you know, you give them enough time with a tape recorder.

259
00:14:23,420 --> 00:14:28,660
I was there when most of them were there, and I said, oh, don't you remember this?

260
00:14:28,740 --> 00:14:30,079
They go, oh, yeah, that happened.

261
00:14:30,319 --> 00:14:34,240
And when you have two guys together, the story comes together a lot clearer.

262
00:14:35,060 --> 00:14:39,839
You know, some of the most memorable parts of scouting are not in the scout handbook.

263
00:14:40,319 --> 00:14:43,760
You talk about one thing, and you talk about it pretty extensively.

264
00:14:43,760 --> 00:14:47,460
So it made an impression on you and your fellow scouts, and that's the geek.

265
00:14:48,120 --> 00:14:54,480
Well, there was a guy named Jack Rankin, and Jack Rankin owned this rubber mask.

266
00:14:55,280 --> 00:14:57,240
It looked like the hunchback of Notre Dame.

267
00:14:57,680 --> 00:14:59,240
The eyes were misshapen.

268
00:14:59,300 --> 00:15:00,280
The face was misshapen.

269
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:05,920
But he brings this rubber mask to the camp out, and if we just looked at that, our imagination just took off.

270
00:15:06,140 --> 00:15:06,460
Uh-huh.

271
00:15:06,720 --> 00:15:08,819
It must have just looked like a piece of gold.

272
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:09,680
Oh, it was.

273
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:17,660
The older guys, my big brother being one of them, you know, one at a time they would take the geek mask out and go raid other campers.

274
00:15:17,959 --> 00:15:19,640
Or, you know, when they were.

275
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:22,120
Richard, you're not supposed to do that.

276
00:15:22,620 --> 00:15:25,780
Well, I watched it.

277
00:15:25,839 --> 00:15:26,359
I didn't do it.

278
00:15:26,400 --> 00:15:27,319
I just watched it.

279
00:15:28,640 --> 00:15:29,920
That's all I can tell about it.

280
00:15:30,280 --> 00:15:37,060
At night, when everybody's settling down and getting ready to sleep, they'd go into other camps and just scare these kids to death.

281
00:15:37,060 --> 00:15:42,640
The funniest part was listening to the screams about a half mile away.

282
00:15:44,219 --> 00:15:46,599
Now, you know, you're not allowed to do that, Richard.

283
00:15:47,439 --> 00:15:48,199
Maybe now.

284
00:15:48,300 --> 00:15:49,380
I don't know how it works.

285
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:51,420
But then it was hard.

286
00:15:51,859 --> 00:15:59,260
The boys that are in my troop today, if you were to present them with that mask today, do you think they would do anything different?

287
00:15:59,459 --> 00:15:59,959
Probably not.

288
00:15:59,960 --> 00:16:05,500
Probably not, but they'd probably be more coordinated because now they've got cell phones.

289
00:16:06,060 --> 00:16:10,740
They can tell you, you know, they can coordinate where the positions are, you know, and stuff like that.

290
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:14,380
And then they've probably got a camera and they could record a video of it and get it online.

291
00:16:14,680 --> 00:16:15,480
I'll put it on YouTube.

292
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:23,580
Another thing I like about the book is it's not about an idealized experience in Boy Scouts.

293
00:16:23,580 --> 00:16:25,720
It's about an experience in Boy Scouts.

294
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:29,600
And that's why I think it rings so true.

295
00:16:30,180 --> 00:16:37,960
But at one point, you as an adult and Mr. Street talked and you expressed to him that you were kind of disappointed that you didn't go that far in Scouts.

296
00:16:38,200 --> 00:16:38,740
That's right.

297
00:16:39,060 --> 00:16:40,580
What was his reply to you?

298
00:16:41,260 --> 00:16:45,220
He said, well, it's not like Scouting is an Eagle factory.

299
00:16:45,220 --> 00:16:48,060
And I thought, you know, he's right.

300
00:16:48,580 --> 00:16:52,560
It's not a factory, you know, to turn people out to be, you know, robots.

301
00:16:52,980 --> 00:16:53,160
Yeah.

302
00:16:53,760 --> 00:16:55,940
And that was another point of the book.

303
00:16:56,020 --> 00:17:01,560
I didn't want kids today, if they ever got a hold of the book, I didn't want them to think that we were perfect.

304
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:04,339
I think that's a really important thing for children to know.

305
00:17:04,880 --> 00:17:11,060
That, you know, all these old people walking around, they had their achievements and disappointments.

306
00:17:11,260 --> 00:17:14,660
And the world is not all that cold and difficult a place.

307
00:17:14,660 --> 00:17:17,940
We had our little scrapes and fights and stuff like that.

308
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:23,340
Which I kind of, you know, we look back now, we go, what were we fighting about?

309
00:17:23,600 --> 00:17:25,560
You know, we should have been just having fun.

310
00:17:26,180 --> 00:17:29,640
I'm looking at page 161, looking toward the future.

311
00:17:29,940 --> 00:17:34,420
Yeah, that first paragraph there, I think it might be a nice way to end our conversation.

312
00:17:35,100 --> 00:17:44,640
Thinking on my days with the Boy Scouts and Troop 826 gives me a sense of gratitude for Mr. Street and the volunteers who helped make our time with Scouting worth remembering.

313
00:17:44,660 --> 00:17:47,620
For me, though, these experiences remain in the past.

314
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:51,300
For any young scout reading this, your experiences are in the future.

315
00:17:51,640 --> 00:17:54,260
If you want, you can go far, perhaps even to Eagle.

316
00:17:54,880 --> 00:18:05,260
I've already pointed out that it's my belief that any scout who makes it that far has, for the most part, learned much of what he needs to know to be successful in his adult life.

317
00:18:05,960 --> 00:18:09,180
If you are willing, go as far as you can in your youth.

318
00:18:09,180 --> 00:18:14,200
What do you think the most important message from your experience is?

319
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:28,660
Well, if a scout troop is led by good men, if the young man can relate to the older men, that if a young man can learn to work with other people, make friends who are going as far as he wants to go, he'll go far.

320
00:18:28,660 --> 00:18:31,880
I certainly do appreciate having talked to you.

321
00:18:32,140 --> 00:18:33,160
Thanks for your interest.

322
00:18:33,480 --> 00:18:35,340
Well, thank you, and thanks for the book.

323
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:36,580
I'm glad you enjoyed it.

324
00:18:36,820 --> 00:18:38,680
I really have. I really, really have.

325
00:18:38,920 --> 00:18:40,160
We'll talk to you again soon.

326
00:18:40,180 --> 00:18:40,900
Okay, Clark.

327
00:18:40,900 --> 00:19:07,400
So this is the third in our series of four little podcast segments about avoiding Eagle Scout drama.

328
00:19:07,400 --> 00:19:19,280
And so far, we've determined that this kind of needless drama can be caused when a scoutmaster seeks to impose his ideal vision of an Eagle Scout on the advancement process.

329
00:19:19,940 --> 00:19:32,560
In addition, we've discovered that Dayton Powell founded scouting on the idea that it would offer scouts a way to challenge themselves towards achievement on an individual basis rather than against an ideal or a numeric standard.

330
00:19:32,560 --> 00:19:43,780
As a rule of thumb in scouting, if there's no numerical metric applied to a requirement, it's improper and against policy to create one.

331
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:48,240
So how in the world can a scoutmaster evaluate scouts fairly?

332
00:19:48,879 --> 00:19:57,679
If scouts challenge themselves against an internalized standard, then it is our task to learn how each scout evaluates their individual performance.

333
00:19:57,680 --> 00:20:02,900
And that's why we have scoutmaster conferences rather than scoutmaster reviews.

334
00:20:04,180 --> 00:20:06,320
Conferring is an exchange of information, right?

335
00:20:06,500 --> 00:20:10,460
A discussion, a series of questions designed to establish a common understanding.

336
00:20:11,480 --> 00:20:18,040
And during conferences for ranks that require leadership tenure, I ask plenty of questions about the scouts' experience with the leadership position.

337
00:20:18,620 --> 00:20:21,960
You know, what was your most and least successful moment as a leader?

338
00:20:22,540 --> 00:20:25,240
What would you change about the way you did the job?

339
00:20:25,240 --> 00:20:29,040
How did you, how do you evaluate your performance?

340
00:20:29,540 --> 00:20:34,460
Do you think you fulfilled the requirement of being an active leader for that period of time?

341
00:20:35,240 --> 00:20:41,680
If I have an issue with the scout's performance and I haven't discussed this with him before the scoutmaster's conference, I'm not doing my job.

342
00:20:41,680 --> 00:20:55,080
If I'm waiting until the scoutmaster's conference, if I'm watching the scout and he's disinterested and he's doing half a job and he's not really getting along very well and, you know, and I say, well, I'm going to get him at the scoutmaster's conference.

343
00:20:55,400 --> 00:20:56,180
Shame on me.

344
00:20:56,440 --> 00:20:57,280
That's an ambush.

345
00:20:57,620 --> 00:20:59,860
And that's, that, that just doesn't cut it.

346
00:20:59,860 --> 00:21:09,520
If I have an ongoing discussion with the scout and he hasn't made any effort to address the issue, well, we'll discuss it further and try to find out what the real problem is.

347
00:21:10,159 --> 00:21:12,560
You have to be responsible as a scoutmaster.

348
00:21:13,260 --> 00:21:18,560
And we have to keep an ongoing dialogue with our scouts that is marked by encouragement rather than fault finding.

349
00:21:18,560 --> 00:21:25,480
They address and resolve concerns and we don't let them become major problems.

350
00:21:26,700 --> 00:21:30,399
Adolescence is a long, infuriating power struggle sometimes.

351
00:21:31,020 --> 00:21:34,659
Our work demands we try to understand the process and not react out of anger.

352
00:21:35,220 --> 00:21:42,460
And we especially don't want to turn a scoutmaster's conference into some kind of sanctimonious exercise of our supposed power.

353
00:21:43,379 --> 00:21:47,679
Scout age boys do a lot of things they shouldn't and don't do a lot of things that they should.

354
00:21:47,680 --> 00:21:50,860
Our role is to work with them and not against them.

355
00:21:51,220 --> 00:21:54,700
To recognize achievement and effort and downplay failure.

356
00:21:55,180 --> 00:21:58,800
And we accomplish this by helping our scouts develop personal goals.

357
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:02,220
Part of a scoutmaster's conference is, you know, what are your goals?

358
00:22:02,320 --> 00:22:03,320
What are you going to do next?

359
00:22:04,340 --> 00:22:10,960
And then to overcome the difficulties they encounter and to deal with disappointment and meet the challenges that they define for themselves.

360
00:22:12,780 --> 00:22:16,600
A scout is going to be a lot harder on himself than you are.

361
00:22:17,680 --> 00:22:28,400
A scout needs to be able to sit there and think about his past performance, what he has done, and then he needs to be able to answer for that.

362
00:22:28,900 --> 00:22:30,700
Be it good, bad, or indifferent.

363
00:22:31,700 --> 00:22:36,940
And if we can teach them that skill, that is something that will follow them for the rest of their lives.

364
00:22:36,940 --> 00:22:43,480
We're going to wrap this up next time with a fourth installment discussing Eagle Scout drama.

